    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a loft addition for a residential project out in Oregon. I'm planning to use Douglas Fir for the main support beams. I'm looking at a span of about 14 feet, and I need to support a live load of 30psf plus the dead load of the floor system itself. Can you help me figure out what size beam I should be looking at to keep deflection within acceptable limits? I want to make sure there's no noticeable bounce once it's finished.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a 14-foot span with Douglas Fir, it's ideal to aim for an L/D ratio (span depth to beam depth) of about 7/24 to minimize deflection. Given your live and dead load requirements, I'd recommend a 8x12-inch Douglas Fir beam with at least 9 inches of bearing. Consulting a structural engineer with your specific floor system dead load and span requirements would give you a precise size based on the I-beam or solid flat shape method. Remember to account for the modified weight (W/D) of the material in your calculations. For any precise calculation and sizing, a professional engineering analysis is necessary to ensure safety and code compliance.",
      "source": "target",
